log loss Histogram-based GPU acceleration Early stopping: 10 rounds Stratified, random state: 42 Pipeline Stage Network Flow Data Binary Check Confidence Action Final Status Resource Usage Appendix B. Example Full Execution Traces The following tables represent the full execution traces for the benign and malicious runs, capturing the decision-making process of the Graph-Based LLM Agent Workflow. Appendix B.1. Benign Run Trace (Normal Traffic) This trace shows the efficiency of the “Early Exit” mechanism. The system correctly identifies the traffic as safe in the first step and bypasses the expensive RAG and LLM analysis stages, as shown in Table A5. Table A5. Full execution trace of benign traffic (Run ID: b9fe6c5f). Bold denotes pipeline stages and field names; italics denote system actions and skipped steps. Details & Output Step 1: Input Processing Protocol: 17,504, Flow Duration: 4.50 s, Total Fwd Packets: 0.69 (Normalized), SYN Flag: −0.88 Step 2: Ensemble Classification Result: Benign (Safe) Benign Confidence: 100.00% (Malicious Probability: 0.00%) Traffic Classified as Safe. Terminating analysis pipeline early. Step 3: Execution Summary Benign. No further analysis required. Skipped: Threat Intelligence Retrieval, SHAP Calculation, LLM Report Generation. Appendix B.2. Malicious Run Trace (Slowloris Attack) This trace demonstrates the full pipeline activation—initial classification, threat intelligence retrieval, SHAP explanation, and final LLM report generation—as shown in Table A6. https://doi.org/10.3390/jsan15020032 J. Sens. Actuator Netw. 2026, 15, 32 33 of 35 Table A6. Full execution trace of malicious traffic (Run ID: 753f26bb). Bold denotes pipeline stages and field names; italics denote system actions and retrieved evidence. Pipeline Stage Network Flow Data Binary Check Multi-Class Prediction Consensus SHAP Feature Analysis Threat Intelligence (RAG) Security Analysis Details & Output Step 1: Input Processing Protocol: 65,184, Flow Duration: −0.447 (No